You're taking the quote out of context; the original discussion wasn't 
even about genocide. It was about what to recommend to a newbie to wish 
for--and specifically, one who wanted to remain relatively unspoiled.

Douglas Henke suggested, "If you're playing unspoiled, one good approach 
is to wish for the most helpful thing you've seen in any of your games 
thus far." Which I think is pretty good advice. Haakon Studebaker 
didn't, and replied with the quote at the top of this post.

My point wasn't that genociding rothes, in general, is a good strategy; 
my point was that using whatever means the RNG has given you to get past 
an obstacle is a good thing. As you learn to deal with the obstacle, 
later, more serious obstacles will surface, and you'll recognize the 
wisdom of keeping your heavier firepower for more serious opponents.
> 
> Now, telling him to genocide soldier ants or plains centaurs or Uruk-hai
> for similar reasons, that's sensible.

What's the difference? They're a valuable source of food as well (well, 
you'd need poison resistance for the ants). The point is that whatever 
is causing *you* trouble in *your* games is what you should focus on.

And of course, if the newbie wants to remain unspoiled, it doesn't make 
sense to suggest genocidable creatures that he may not even have 
encountered yet.

